{"name":"Blue Leviathan","hex":"#032a62","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"leviathan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vast sea monster of tremendous strength, either imaginary or real, described as the most dangerous and powerful creature in the ocean.","A thing which is monstrously great in size, strength, etc. (especially a ship); also, a person with great power or wealth."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very large; enormous, gargantuan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leviathan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Leviathan","blocks":[{"text":"Leviathan is a giant sea serpent noted in theology and mythology. It is referenced in the Hebrew Bible, as a metaphor for a powerful enemy, notably Babylon. It is referred to in Psalms, the Book of Job, the Book of Isaiah, and the Book of Enoch. Leviathan is often an embodiment of chaos, threatening to eat the damned when their lives are over. In the end, it is annihilated. Christian theologians identified Leviathan with the demon of the deadly sin envy. According to Ophite Diagrams, Leviathan encapsulates the space of the material world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Destruction_of_Leviathan.png/330px-Destruction_of_Leviathan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leviathan","wikidata":"Q192677"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/500px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/1280px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/500px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The circular salt sea Tiamat (green) and sphere of cosmic freshwater ocean Abzu are pre-existent deities in Sumerian myths, from whose mating Earth was created, so the sketch's side view shows the same as Babylon's map. Referring to the Atra-Hasis epic, Abzu is the celestial reservoir from which an organisation of younger gods triggers the Flood in order to consume humanity – also a source for the dangerous cosmic sea monster Leviathan.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABabylon's_world_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg/500px-Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg/500px-Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leviathan the sea-monster, with Behemoth the land-monster and Ziz the air-monster. \"And on that day were two monsters parted, a female monster named Leviathan, to dwell in the abysses of the ocean over the fountains of the waters. But the male is named Behemoth, who occupied with his breast a waste wilderness named Duidain.\" (1 Enoch 60:7–8)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALev-Beh-Ziz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/500px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/1280px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/500px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leviathan (1983), a painting by Michael Sgan-Cohen, the Israel Museum Collection, Jerusalem","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%